#LyX version: 2.5.2
#
#   This is a definition file of a color theme "D42ker" of LyX
#
#   A clean, minimal theme: zero-saturation neutral scale, one warm red accent,
#   a few muted hues reserved for math, labels and change tracking. Inspired by
#   the D42ker GitHub theme for Typora
#   (Leon Wong, github.com/l3ony2k/Typora-D42ker-Github-theme).
#
#   Palette                light      dark
#     background           #fdfdfd    #191919
#     surface              #f2f2f2    #212121
#     surface raised       #e8e8e8    #2a2a2a
#     rule / frame         #dcdcdc    #404040
#     muted                #9a9a9a    #808080
#     dim                  #6e6e6e    #b4b4b4
#     foreground           #262626    #d0d0d0
#     accent red           #ac0024    #feadaa     h  22
#     blue                 #005da0    #8ec6ff     h 249
#     green                #006f1a    #86d489     h 145
#     plum                 #8e1a96    #f2a9f6     h 325
#     orange               #8b4500    #feb079     h  55
#     teal                 #006869    #26d5d7     h 196
#     note background      #f6f1e3    #2b2721
#
#   Accents are built in OKLCH at the highest in-gamut chroma (C 0.13-0.16).
#   Lightness is set by APCA rather than by eye. LyX draws most labels in a
#   Small font, so both sides target the small-text band: light sits at L=0.47
#   (Lc 80-84), dark is raised until |Lc| >= 68. WCAG 2 ratios flatter dark
#   themes badly -- these read 5:1 there at lightnesses APCA scores near 40.
#   The hues match the consensus of Modus, Catppuccin, Everforest, Solarized
#   and Primer, all of which place blue at 236-263, red 20-29, orange 40-59
#   and green 118-148 in OKLCH.
#
#   The five change-tracking author colors keep those hues but are staggered in
#   lightness, so they stay separable under protanopia, deuteranopia and
#   tritanopia (simulated with Brettel 1997), where hue alone collapses.
#
#   Order below: canvas, selection, buttons/frames, insets, notes, layout
#   markers, math, labels, change tracking, feedback, tables.
#
#   Note: "selectionmath" is the *text* color of selected math, not a
#   background (the background is "selection"); "deletedtextmodifier" is a
#   brightness a deleted-text color is blended toward, not a color as such.
#
#   Author: Tom Bleher ()
#
#   Syntax: \set_color "color name" "light color" "dark color"
#
\set_color "background" "#fdfdfd" "#191919"
\set_color "foreground" "#262626" "#d0d0d0"
\set_color "foreground_inverted" "#fdfdfd" "#191919"
\set_color "cursor" "#262626" "#d0d0d0"
\set_color "cursor_inverted" "#fdfdfd" "#191919"
